# Dependencies and build artifacts.
#
# BOTH patterns are required, and the second is the one that was missing. Docker matches
# .dockerignore against the path from the CONTEXT ROOT, so a bare `node_modules` excludes
# `./node_modules` and nothing else. `docs-site/node_modules` was therefore copied in whole:
#
#     local build:  /app/docs-site  249 MB  (240 MB of it node_modules)
#     CI build:     absent
#
# CI never saw it because CI checks out clean -- so a local build and a CI build produced
# materially different images and nothing reported the difference. `**/node_modules` catches any
# depth, including any future workspace.
node_modules
**/node_modules
build

# Local runtime state.
#
# `recipes.json` is the saved-recipe STORE. A developer who builds this image locally bakes their
# own saved recipes into a layer -- and would publish them by pushing it. Mode 0600 on disk stops
# mattering the moment the file is inside an image.
#
# Found by listing /app in a locally-built image, where both files were sitting beside the source.
recipes.json
recipes.json.backup
*.recipes.json

# Generated files (regenerated during build by grunt configTests)
src/core/config/OperationConfig.json
src/core/config/modules/
src/node/index.mjs

docker-compose*.yml

# Development files
.git
.github
.vscode
.idea

# Documentation (copied during build, removed in Dockerfile.mcp)
*.md
!package.json

# Test artifacts. `tests/` itself is NOT excluded, deliberately.
#
# `src/core/config/scripts/generateOpsIndex.mjs` (upstream's file, byte-identical) writes two
# indexes: src/core/operations/index.mjs and tests/operations/index.mjs. It readdir's
# tests/operations/tests/ unconditionally, so excluding `tests` from the build context makes
# `npx grunt configTests` die with ENOENT.
#
# That was previously invisible: the Gruntfile chained its config scripts with `;`, so the chain's
# exit status was the trailing `echo` and the failure was swallowed -- the image built "green" with
# the ops index already written and the error discarded. Chaining with `&&` surfaced it.
#
# The image is unaffected either way: Dockerfile.mcp's builder stage `rm -rf`s `tests` before the
# runtime stage copies /app, so this costs 3.7 MB of build context and nothing in the final image.
# Excluding it instead would mean hand-editing an upstream-mirrored file, which the next sync
# reverts.

# Test and coverage OUTPUT, as opposed to the `tests` sources above. These are produced by a local
# `npm run test:coverage` / `npm test` run and are pure build-context weight -- nothing in the
# image reads them, and a stale local copy would be silently baked into the layer.
coverage
.nyc_output
test-results

# The upstream reference checkout. A populated `ref-proj/CyberChef` is a second full copy of the
# source tree; it exists only so the sync can three-way diff against upstream and has no business
# in a build context. Spotted by listing /app in the built image, where it had survived because
# the builder stage's `rm -rf` list never named it.
ref-proj

# Trees with no runtime use. The builder stage removes some of these, but excluding them from the
# CONTEXT is strictly better: what is never copied cannot be forgotten in a later edit of that
# rm -rf list, and it makes every build faster.
#
# `deploy/`, `examples/`, `patches/` and `docs-site/` are read by humans and CI, never by the
# server. `images/` is 8 MB of banner JPEGs and the Helm chart icon.
docs-site
deploy
examples
patches
images
.agy-review-work

# IDE and OS files
*.log
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Temporary files
tmp
temp
*.tmp
